1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,007
Lesson 2.8.

2
00:00:02,387 --> 00:00:05,206
Continuous-time Markov jump processes.

3
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:09,413
Not every noisy system moves continuously.

4
00:00:09,793 --> 00:00:13,774
A pbit jumps between minus one
and plus one; a molecule is

5
00:00:13,774 --> 00:00:17,961
produced or degraded; an electron
enters or leaves an island.

6
00:00:18,341 --> 00:00:21,842
These are continuous-time
jump processes: the state is

7
00:00:21,842 --> 00:00:25,085
discrete, but the jumps
happen at random instants.

8
00:00:25,465 --> 00:00:28,519
They are the language of
Brownian circuits and of the

9
00:00:28,519 --> 00:00:31,516
chemical reaction networks
we will see in unit five.

10
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:36,732
Think of a pbit as a coin that,
every now and then, flips itself.

11
00:00:37,112 --> 00:00:41,559
From state minus one it jumps
to plus one with rate k₊, that

12
00:00:41,559 --> 00:00:45,414
is, with probability k₊ dt
in each tiny interval dt.

13
00:00:45,794 --> 00:00:48,329
From plus one it returns with rate k₋.

14
00:00:48,709 --> 00:00:52,366
The waiting time in each state
is exponential and memoryless.

15
00:00:52,746 --> 00:00:59,166
In the long run, the fraction of time
in state plus one is k₊/(k₊ + k₋).

16
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:03,439
For the full distribution,
each state gains probability

17
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:06,594
from incoming jumps and loses
it through outgoing ones.

18
00:01:06,974 --> 00:01:13,195
That is the master equation:
dp_i/dt = Σ_j (p_j Q_ji − p_i Q_ij).

19
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:21,050
In matrix form, ṗ = p Q, where Q is the
rate matrix, whose rows sum to zero.

20
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:26,408
It is the continuous-time
version of p_{n+1} = p_n

21
00:01:26,408 --> 00:01:30,588
P, and its solution is p(t) = p(0) e^(Qt).

22
00:01:30,968 --> 00:01:35,105
The bars show how the distribution
relaxes toward the stationary state.

23
00:01:36,617 --> 00:01:39,862
How do we simulate a trajectory
without discretizing time?

24
00:01:40,242 --> 00:01:42,647
Gillespie proposed two draws per event.

25
00:01:43,027 --> 00:01:46,435
First, the time to the
next jump, exponential

26
00:01:46,435 --> 00:01:49,844
with rate equal to the
sum of all exit rates.

27
00:01:50,224 --> 00:01:54,613
Second, which jump happens, with
probability proportional to its rate.

28
00:01:54,993 --> 00:01:57,822
It is exact: there is no
time step and no bias.

29
00:01:58,202 --> 00:02:00,899
The animation applies it to a molecule

30
00:02:00,899 --> 00:02:04,023
produced at rate k and
degraded at rate γ n.

31
00:02:04,403 --> 00:02:07,116
The histogram of the number of molecules

32
00:02:07,116 --> 00:02:10,509
converges to a Poisson
distribution with mean k/γ.

33
00:02:11,617 --> 00:02:14,195
Many computing questions
are first-passage questions:

34
00:02:14,575 --> 00:02:17,628
how long does the system take to
reach a state for the first time?

35
00:02:18,008 --> 00:02:22,241
For example, how long a Brownian
circuit takes to complete a computation.

36
00:02:22,621 --> 00:02:28,866
If the computation requires N successive
jumps, each with rate k, the total

37
00:02:28,866 --> 00:02:34,779
time is a sum of N exponentials: its
mean is N/k and its variance N/k².

38
00:02:35,159 --> 00:02:38,767
The relative precision
improves as one over root N.

39
00:02:39,147 --> 00:02:42,658
The Gillespie histograms
narrow as N grows.

40
00:02:43,038 --> 00:02:47,482
In unit three we will see that this
precision costs entropy production.

41
00:02:48,583 --> 00:02:53,401
Production at rate ten per second
and degradation of one per second per

42
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:58,355
molecule: the mean number at steady
state is ten, with variance also ten.

43
00:02:58,735 --> 00:03:02,782
For the pbit with k₊ = 3
and k₋ = 1 per microsecond,

44
00:03:02,782 --> 00:03:06,362
the probability of plus
one is three quarters,

45
00:03:06,742 --> 00:03:10,828
and the autocorrelation decays
with rate k₊ + k₋, four per

46
00:03:10,828 --> 00:03:15,406
microsecond: a correlation time
of two hundred fifty nanoseconds.

47
00:03:16,517 --> 00:03:22,146
Jump processes describe pbit updates on
a chip, Brownian token circuits whose

48
00:03:22,146 --> 00:03:27,483
computation time is a first-passage
time, and chemical reaction networks.

49
00:03:27,863 --> 00:03:32,546
In unit three we will use them to state
thermodynamic uncertainty relations.

50
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:37,530
Frequent mistakes: confusing
a rate with a probability.

51
00:03:37,910 --> 00:03:41,069
Forgetting that the rows of
the rate matrix sum to zero.

52
00:03:41,449 --> 00:03:44,353
Discretizing time when Gillespie is exact.

53
00:03:44,733 --> 00:03:48,722
And reporting only the mean first-passage
time, without its variability.

54
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:54,198
In summary: a jump process
is described by rates; the

55
00:03:54,198 --> 00:03:58,053
distribution obeys the
master equation ṗ = p Q.

56
00:03:58,433 --> 00:04:02,281
Gillespie simulates exact
trajectories with two draws per event.

57
00:04:02,661 --> 00:04:06,529
And first-passage times, with their
mean and variance, measure how

58
00:04:06,529 --> 00:04:09,928
long a stochastic computation
takes and how precise it is.

59
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:15,495
To practice: solve the pbit master
equation, prove that the Poisson

60
00:04:15,495 --> 00:04:19,609
distribution is stationary,
compute the mean and variance of a

61
00:04:19,609 --> 00:04:24,519
first-passage time, and describe the
two draws of the Gillespie algorithm.
